<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ka Mate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">around <a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Ka Mate&quot; is a Māori haka composed by Te Rauparaha, the leader of the iwi (tribe) of Ngāti Toa of the North Island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">Musket</a> Wars. Around 1820, pursued by enemies of Ngāti Maniapoto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a>, he hid on Motuopihi Island in Lake Rotoaira inside a kūmara (sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a>) storage pit, while a woman named Rangikoaea sat astride the pit to hide and protect him.</p></div>
